// What this package claims, checked where possible against somebody else's brotli.
//
// **The fixtures below were produced by the Rust `brotli` crate**, which is a from-scratch port
// rather than a binding of the C library this package calls — so a stream in this file agreeing with
// the plaintext beside it says the *format* was read, not that one library agrees with itself. Each
// was additionally offered to the `brotli` command line before being written down, and both accepted
// it; where those two had disagreed the right move would have been a third encoder, per the lesson
// qrcodegen's suite carries.
//
// **The encode direction cannot be pinned the same way in a unit test**, because a compressed stream
// is a *choice* rather than an answer and every encoder makes a different one — the Rust crate and
// the C library produce different bytes for the same input at the same quality, and both are
// correct. What stands in for it is that `libbrotlienc` and `libbrotlidec` are two separate
// libraries: a round trip here is one of them checking the other, not code checking itself. Our
// output was also handed to the Rust decoder and to the command line outside the suite; the README
// records that.

module sh.sysl.brotli
@tests

import sysl.harness.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// -- Fixtures from an independent implementation ---------------------------------------------------

private short_plain() -> []const u8 = [
    104, 101, 108, 108, 111, 44, 32, 98, 114, 111, 116, 108, 105
]

// Produced by the Rust `brotli` crate at quality 5 — 17 bytes.
private short_br() -> []const u8 = [
    11, 6, 128, 104, 101, 108, 108, 111, 44, 32, 98, 114, 111, 116, 108, 105, 3
]

